      title: 'Appropriations',
      tags: ['TWELVE REGULAR BILLS', 'ONE PER SUBCOMMITTEE', 'A CR WHEN LATE'],
      body: `Appropriations bills provide the <b>budget authority</b> that lets federal agencies spend money. Twelve House Appropriations subcommittees each develop one regular bill a year. A bill is marked up in subcommittee, reported by the full committee, passed by the House and then considered and amended by the Senate, which usually acts second. Both chambers must pass the same text before it goes to the President.

Bills are often combined: a <b>minibus</b> holds some, but not most or all, of them, and an <b>omnibus</b> is one vehicle for many.

Budget authority typically expires September 30. If bills are not enacted by then, a <b>continuing resolution</b> provides interim funding, usually at the prior year's rate, with limits and exceptions called <b>anomalies</b>. With neither, funding lapses: the <b>Antideficiency Act</b> generally bars spending and affected agencies must cease most operations.`,
